<h1>Video 057 — Overriding and var-arg methods</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java With OCJP/SCJP: OOPs(Object Oriented Programming) Part-7||overriding ||varargs method</p>
<p>ASR decoded while reading the captions: wear / we Arch / where AR / VCH / wech / aarch / verar / V meod = var-arg methods; keep subing / keep supporting / keep supping = keep writing (board heading); into / INX / inex / Intex = int x; into dot dot dot X = int... x; public W / y / wi / War / vo / wide / vo vo M1 = public void m1; tri8 / 38 / trible 8 / 8 = 888; tri9 / 39 / 9 / 98 (as “9 then 8”) = 999 then 888; jum / JM = JVM; written types = return types; Co variant = covariant; polymeris / polymer ISM = polymorphism; whoops = OOPs; string ARR a = String[] args; Javas C test.Java = javac Test.java; PKA / Paka = pakka (definite); I exception / C exception = IOException; AR / VAR / aist / erist list = ArrayList; Linker / link list = LinkedList; list ENT = List interface; get Z / get off zero = get(0); subal lakmi / subaki / appal = Subbalakshmi; Nara / charmi / charm / Trisha = Nayanthara / Charmi / Trisha; method of hiding / iding = method hiding; Legion = listen.</p>
<p>This video is OOPs Part 7. Last session finished static methods (mix static/non-static is CE; both static is method hiding, output parent / child / parent). This session: overriding with respect to var-arg methods (var-arg vs normal is overloading, output parent / child / parent; both var-arg is overriding, output parent / child / child), then overriding with respect to variables (always 888 999 888, compiler + reference type, four static/instance combinations), then nine differences overloading vs overriding plus the “check only two things vs check everything” note, then an exam-style parent/child method question (five options), then polymorphism (definition, life story, abs overloading, marry overriding, List parent-ref), then when to use parent vs child reference (get(0) returns Object; List as return type) with a three-row interview table, then three pillars of OOPs, a static vs dynamic polymorphism summary (overloading + method hiding vs overriding), and the internet “friendship” definition. Next session: coupling, cohesion, type casting, instance/static control flow, constructors.</p>
<h3>00:05 — Recap: last session was static methods; this session is var-arg</h3>
<p>Last session: overriding with respect to static methods — that chapter of overriding rules is complete.</p>
<p>Now: overriding with respect to var-arg methods (ASR: “wear methods” / “we Arch methods”).</p>
<p>He does not re-teach what a var-arg method is. He hopes the room already knows it from Language Fundamentals (video 012). He only needs a one-line reminder so the overriding rule makes sense.</p>
<h3>00:26 — Board heading: Overriding with respect to var-arg methods</h3>
<p>Keep writing (ASR: “keep subing”):</p>
<p>Overriding with respect to var-arg methods</p>
<h3>00:48 — Reminder: var-arg came in 1.5</h3>
<p>A new concept in 1.5: var-arg methods (variable number of argument methods).</p>
<p>Normal method — exactly one int:</p>
<pre class='code'>class NormalOneInt {
    public void m1(int x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;exactly one int&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NormalOneInt t = new NormalOneInt();
        t.m1(10);
        // prints exactly one int
        // t.m1();        // CE: m1(int) not applicable
        // t.m1(10, 20);  // CE: m1(int) not applicable
    }
}</pre>
<p>Var-arg method — any number of int values, including zero:</p>
<pre class='code'>class VarArgAnyCount {
    public void m1(int... x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;var-arg method&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        VarArgAnyCount t = new VarArgAnyCount();
        t.m1();
        t.m1(10);
        t.m1(10, 20);
        t.m1(10, 20, 30);
        // prints var-arg method
        // prints var-arg method
        // prints var-arg method
        // prints var-arg method
    }
}</pre>
<p>He writes m1(int... x) on the board (ASR: “M1 off into dot dot dot X”). That is the 1.5 concept. Now the overriding rule.</p>
<h3>01:40 — Board: parent var-arg `m1`, child **normal** `m1`</h3>
<p>Take special care. Parent class P: var-arg method. Child class C extends P: normal method with the same name.</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1(int... x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1(int x) { // normal method, not var-arg
        System.out.println(&quot;child&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<p>Parent method = var-arg. Child method = normal. Same name m1. He is about to put them in class Test with the usual three reference/object cases from the overriding videos.</p>
<h3>02:41 — `class Test`: three calls `m1(10)`</h3>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1(int... x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1(int x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;child&quot;);
    }
}

class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p = new P();
        p.m1(10);

        C c = new C();
        c.m1(10);

        P p1 = new C();
        p1.m1(10);
    }
}</pre>
<p>Target: parent method is var-arg; child method is normal. Three cases, each passing one argument 10.</p>
<h3>03:57 — Cases 1 and 2 are easy; case 3 is the trap</h3>
<p>Case 1 — parent reference, parent object</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1(int... x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent&quot;);
    }
}

class Case1 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p = new P();
        p.m1(10);
        // prints parent
    }
}</pre>
<p>Parent reference, parent object → parent method. No argument.</p>
<p>Case 2 — child reference, child object</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1(int... x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1(int x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;child&quot;);
    }
}

class Case2 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        C c = new C();
        c.m1(10);
        // prints child
    }
}</pre>
<p>Child reference, child object → child method. No argument.</p>
<p>Case 3 — parent reference, child object (the dangerous one, same pattern as marry() in video 055)</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1(int... x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1(int x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;child&quot;);
    }
}

class Case3Trap {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p1 = new C();
        p1.m1(10);
        // most of the room shouts &quot;child&quot;
        // actual: prints parent
    }
}</pre>
<p>He asks the room: parent or child? Very important. People shout both. He says some explanation must be required — do not just guess.</p>
<h3>04:46 — Why the room says child (the overriding reflex)</h3>
<p>In overriding, method resolution is always taken care of by the JVM based on runtime object.</p>
<p>Runtime object of p1 is a child object. So the reflex answer is: child method should execute.</p>
<p>He then stops them: who told you this is the overriding concept?</p>
<h3>05:16 — Answer is **parent**, not child</h3>
<p>The answer is parent, not child.</p>
<p>Most people will feel “parent reference + child object → child” like the marry() demo. That is wrong here. It is not child. Parent is the answer.</p>
<h3>05:32 — Who told you it is overriding? Argument types are **different** → overloading</h3>
<p>In overriding: method names must be same and argument types must be same.</p>
<p>Here:</p>
<ul><li>parent argument: int... x</li></ul>
<ul><li>child argument: int x (normal)</li></ul>
<p>Same name, different argument types.</p>
<p>If two methods have the same name but different argument types, that is overloading, not overriding.</p>
<p>Most people fail this: they call it overriding. It is overloading.</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1(int... x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1(int x) { // different argument type than int...
        System.out.println(&quot;child&quot;);
    }
}

class NotOverriding {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p1 = new C();
        p1.m1(10);
        // prints parent
        // overloading, but not overriding
        // compiler binds using reference type P → P.m1(int...)
    }
}</pre>
<h3>06:37 — Rule: a var-arg method can be overridden with **another var-arg method only**</h3>
<p>Board rule (he will dictate it slowly later):</p>
<p>A var-arg method can be overridden with another var-arg method only.</p>
<p>If you try to “override” it with a normal method, it becomes overloading but not overriding.</p>
<p>In overloading, method resolution is always taken care of by the compiler based on reference type.</p>
<p>So on the three calls:</p>
<p>Take special care: we cannot override a var-arg method with a normal method.</p>
<h3>07:37 — If child is **also** var-arg, then it **is** overriding → child on case 3</h3>
<p>Suppose the child method is also m1(int... x). Now both are var-arg. Now it becomes overriding. Case 3 answer becomes child.</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1(int... x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1(int... x) { // also var-arg → real override
        System.out.println(&quot;child&quot;);
    }
}

class BothVarArg {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p = new P();
        p.m1(10);
        // prints parent

        C c = new C();
        c.m1(10);
        // prints child

        P p1 = new C();
        p1.m1(10);
        // prints child  — JVM, runtime object C
    }
}</pre>
<p>He will execute the first program (child = normal) live, then talk about this change.</p>
<h3>08:21 — Live: save as `Test.java`, `javac Test.java`, `java Test`</h3>
<p>He opens the same example: class P public void m1(int... x) parent method; class C extends P public void m1(int x) normal method; class Test with the three calls.</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1(int... x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1(int x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;child&quot;);
    }
}

class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p = new P();
        p.m1(10);

        C c = new C();
        c.m1(10);

        P p1 = new C();
        p1.m1(10);
    }
}</pre>
<p>Commands (ASR: “Javas C test.Java”):</p>
<p>javac Test.java
java Test</p>
<p>Compiles. Output, he asks before running:</p>
<ol><li>first time → parent</li></ol>
<ol><li>second time → child</li></ol>
<ol><li>third time → parent</li></ol>
<p>Observed output: parent child parent.</p>
<h3>09:38 — Same live file, child also var-arg → parent child child</h3>
<p>Suppose child class method is also a var-arg method. What is the answer now?</p>
<p>Parent, child, child. Because now it is overriding.</p>
<p>Default output of the first program (normal child): parent child parent.</p>
<p>If both var-arg: parent child child.</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1(int... x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1(int... x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;child&quot;);
    }
}

class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p = new P();
        p.m1(10);

        C c = new C();
        c.m1(10);

        P p1 = new C();
        p1.m1(10);
        // prints parent
        // prints child
        // prints child
    }
}</pre>
<p>We can override a var-arg method with another var-arg method only. By mistake if we try to override with a normal method, it becomes overloading but not overriding.</p>
<h3>10:17 — Board dictation of the rule (copy slowly)</h3>
<p>Keep writing. He repeats each phrase several times:</p>
<ul><li>We can override var-arg method with another var-arg method only.</li></ul>
<ul><li>If we are trying to override with the normal method, then it will become overloading but not overriding.</li></ul>
<p>That is the whole rule.</p>
<h3>11:45 — Classroom: “we passed only one argument — still overloading?”</h3>
<p>Student: if it is a normal method in both classes, then it is overriding (both normal, same int).</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1(int x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1(int x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;child&quot;);
    }
}

class BothNormal {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p = new P();
        p.m1(10);
        C c = new C();
        c.m1(10);
        P p1 = new C();
        p1.m1(10);
        // prints parent
        // prints child
        // prints child  — this IS overriding
    }
}</pre>
<p>Follow-up: here we passed only one argument. Does that make it overriding?</p>
<p>No. Even though we pass one argument, overloading still dominates because the declared argument types are different (int... vs int). Reference type decides which method is called.</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1(int... x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1(int x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;child&quot;);
    }
}

class OneArgStillOverload {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p1 = new C();
        p1.m1(10); // still only one int, still overloading
        // prints parent
    }
}</pre>
<h3>12:39 — If parent **also** declared the normal `m1(int)`, that one would win</h3>
<p>This case is overloading. Compiler, reference type.</p>
<p>On P p1 = new C(); p1.m1(10); the parent type has only m1(int...), so that is the only method that can get the chance.</p>
<p>If parent also had the exact-match normal method, that method would get the chance instead of the var-arg (var-arg is weakest / least priority — same idea as video 012).</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1(int... x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent var-arg&quot;);
    }

    public void m1(int x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent normal&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1(int x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;child&quot;);
    }
}

class ParentAlsoHasNormal {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p1 = new C();
        p1.m1(10);
        // prints child
        // P has m1(int) as well → that is the overload chosen (exact match)
        // C overrides m1(int) → runtime object C → child
    }
}</pre>
<p>Take clearly. Comment on the original program: overloading but not overriding.</p>
<h3>13:33 — Full board program again, with the comment and the output line</h3>
<p>He rewrites the first program cleanly and writes the output parent child parent.</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1(int... x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1(int x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;child&quot;);
    }
}

class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p = new P();
        p.m1(10);

        C c = new C();
        c.m1(10);

        P p1 = new C();
        p1.m1(10);
        // overloading but not overriding
        // prints parent
        // prints child
        // prints parent
    }
}</pre>
<h3>14:36 — In the above program, replace child method with var-arg → overriding</h3>
<p>Keep writing:</p>
<p>In the above program, if we replace child method with var-arg method, then it will become overriding.</p>
<p>Because we are replacing the normal child method with a var-arg method. In this case the output is parent child child (he first slips “parent child parent”, then corrects: sorry, parent child child).</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1(int... x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1(int... x) { // replaced normal with var-arg
        System.out.println(&quot;child&quot;);
    }
}

class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p = new P();
        p.m1(10);

        C c = new C();
        c.m1(10);

        P p1 = new C();
        p1.m1(10);
        // now overriding
        // prints parent
        // prints child
        // prints child
    }
}</pre>
<p>This is what you should be aware of: overriding with respect to var-arg methods.</p>
<h3>16:06 — Next heading: Overriding with respect to **variables**</h3>
<p>Keep writing:</p>
<p>Overriding with respect to variables</p>
<p>This example is also a bit important. Take special care.</p>
<h3>16:42 — Board: parent `x = 888`, child `x = 999`</h3>
<p>He starts to write int x = 10, then changes to a “strong value”: 888 and 999 (ASR: “tri8” / “38” / “888”; “tri9” / “39” / “999”). Same name x.</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    int x = 888;
}

class C extends P {
    int x = 999;
}</pre>
<p>Parent contains 888. Child contains 999. Same x only.</p>
<h3>17:29 — `class Test`: print `p.x`, `c.x`, `p1.x`</h3>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    int x = 888;
}

class C extends P {
    int x = 999;
}

class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p = new P();
        System.out.println(p.x);

        C c = new C();
        System.out.println(c.x);

        P p1 = new C();
        System.out.println(p1.x);
    }
}</pre>
<h3>18:38 — Trap: people apply the **method** rule to **variables**</h3>
<ul><li>p.x — parent reference, parent object → 888</li></ul>
<ul><li>c.x — child reference, child object → 999</li></ul>
<ul><li>p1.x — parent reference, child object</li></ul>
<p>The room applies overriding: method resolution is based on runtime object; both are instance variables (not static); runtime object is child → they shout 999.</p>
<p>He says the answer is 888. (ASR: “trible 8 is the answer.”)</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    int x = 888;
}

class C extends P {
    int x = 999;
}

class VariableTrap {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p1 = new C();
        System.out.println(p1.x);
        // room expects 999 (runtime object C)
        // prints 888
    }
}</pre>
<h3>19:40 — First point: overriding is applicable **only for methods**, not for variables</h3>
<p>Who told you it is overriding?</p>
<p>Overriding concept is applicable only for methods but not for variables.</p>
<p>If it were a method, method resolution would be based on runtime object. This is not overriding, because overriding does not apply to variables.</p>
<h3>20:11 — Variable resolution: compiler, reference type</h3>
<p>Variable resolution always takes care by compiler based on reference type.</p>
<p>Whether the variable is instance or static, there is no change. The value you get is always from the reference type.</p>
<p>So:</p>
<ul><li>parent reference → 888</li></ul>
<ul><li>child reference → 999</li></ul>
<ul><li>parent reference (even with child object) → 888</li></ul>
<h3>20:34 — Four combinations: static / non-static matrix</h3>
<p>He lists all four. The rule does not change.</p>
<p>1. Both instance (non-static)</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    int x = 888;
}

class C extends P {
    int x = 999;
}

class BothInstance {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p = new P();
        System.out.println(p.x);
        C c = new C();
        System.out.println(c.x);
        P p1 = new C();
        System.out.println(p1.x);
        // prints 888
        // prints 999
        // prints 888
    }
}</pre>
<p>2. Parent static, child non-static</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    static int x = 888;
}

class C extends P {
    int x = 999;
}

class ParentStaticChildInstance {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p = new P();
        System.out.println(p.x);
        C c = new C();
        System.out.println(c.x);
        P p1 = new C();
        System.out.println(p1.x);
        // prints 888
        // prints 999
        // prints 888
    }
}</pre>
<p>3. Parent non-static, child static</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    int x = 888;
}

class C extends P {
    static int x = 999;
}

class ParentInstanceChildStatic {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p = new P();
        System.out.println(p.x);
        C c = new C();
        System.out.println(c.x);
        P p1 = new C();
        System.out.println(p1.x);
        // prints 888
        // prints 999
        // prints 888
    }
}</pre>
<p>4. Both static</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    static int x = 888;
}

class C extends P {
    static int x = 999;
}

class BothStatic {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p = new P();
        System.out.println(p.x);
        C c = new C();
        System.out.println(c.x);
        P p1 = new C();
        System.out.println(p1.x);
        // prints 888
        // prints 999
        // prints 888
    }
}</pre>
<p>Whether static/non-static, non-static/static, or static/static — no change in the rule. Variable resolution is always compiler + reference type. In all these cases the output is 888 999 888.</p>
<h3>22:48 — Board dictation: variable resolution (copy slowly)</h3>
<p>Keep writing:</p>
<ul><li>Variable resolution always takes care by compiler based on reference type</li></ul>
<ul><li>irrespective of whether the variable is static or non-static</li></ul>
<ul><li>(in brackets) Overriding concept applicable only for methods but not for variables</li></ul>
<h3>24:31 — Live execution of all four combinations</h3>
<p>Same Test as the board: P.x = 888, C.x = 999, print p.x c.x p1.x.</p>
<p>Both instance → 888 999 888.</p>
<p>Parent static, child non-static — no problem, same answer 888 999 888.</p>
<p>Parent non-static, child static — same output 888 999 888.</p>
<p>Both static — same output 888 999 888.</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    int x = 888; // he then toggles static on P and/or C; output never changes
}

class C extends P {
    int x = 999;
}

class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p = new P();
        System.out.println(p.x);
        C c = new C();
        System.out.println(c.x);
        P p1 = new C();
        System.out.println(p1.x);
        // prints 888
        // prints 999
        // prints 888
    }
}</pre>
<p>Irrespective of static or non-static, no change in the rule.</p>
<h3>26:10 — Take the example **and the table**</h3>
<p>Long board-copy pause (~26:27–28:06) while the room copies the four-row table.</p>
<p>Overriding concept applicable only for methods but not for variables. Make sure you are aware.</p>
<p>These are the various OOPs points you should know related to overriding. Overriding rules are now complete.</p>
<h3>28:33 — Interview: difference between overloading and overriding</h3>
<p>Very common, very important interview question: what is the difference between overloading and overriding? Tell all differences. There may be a chance they ask you to differentiate.</p>
<p>Overriding-specific rules are done. Now generalized points that cover both.</p>
<h3>28:58 — Board heading: Differences between overloading and overriding</h3>
<p>Keep writing:</p>
<p>Differences between overloading and overriding</p>
<p>Three columns: property | overloading | overriding</p>
<p>He will go point by point (nine points), then dictate the whole table slowly.</p>
<h3>30:14 — Property 1: method names</h3>
<p>Method names — must be same or different?</p>
<p>Must be same in both overloading and overriding.</p>
<pre class='code'>class OverloadNames {
    public void m1(int i) { }
    public void m1(float f) { } // same name
}

class P {
    public void m1() {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1() { // same name
        System.out.println(&quot;child&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<h3>30:43 — Property 2: argument types</h3>
<p>Overloading: must be different (at least order). Example he says: int, float vs float, int — change the order at least.</p>
<p>Overriding: must be same including order.</p>
<pre class='code'>class OverloadAtLeastOrder {
    public void m1(int i, float f) {
        System.out.println(&quot;int, float&quot;);
    }

    public void m1(float f, int i) {
        System.out.println(&quot;float, int&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        OverloadAtLeastOrder t = new OverloadAtLeastOrder();
        t.m1(10, 10.5f);
        t.m1(10.5f, 10);
        // prints int, float
        // prints float, int
    }
}
class P {
    public void m1(int i, float f) {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1(int i, float f) { // same types including order
        System.out.println(&quot;child&quot;);
    }
}

class OverrideSameOrder {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p1 = new C();
        p1.m1(10, 10.5f);
        // prints child
    }
}</pre>
<h3>31:33 — Property 3: method signatures</h3>
<p>Method name + argument types = method signature.</p>
<p>Overloading: signatures must be different (because argument types are different).</p>
<p>Overriding: signatures must be same.</p>
<pre class='code'>class OverloadDifferentSignatures {
    public void m1(int i) { }   // m1(int)
    public void m1(float f) { } // m1(float) — different signature
}

class P {
    public void m1(int i) { } // m1(int)
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1(int i) { } // m1(int) — same signature
}</pre>
<h3>32:18 — Property 4: return types</h3>
<p>Overloading: we never worry about return type. Maybe same, maybe different. No restrictions. Check only: names same, arguments different.</p>
<p>Overriding: must be same until 1.4 version. From 1.5 version onwards, covariant return types are also allowed.</p>
<pre class='code'>class OverloadReturnIgnored {
    public void m1(int i) { }
    public int m1(double d) { // different args → return type free
        return 10;
    }
}
class P {
    public Object m1() {
        return null;
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public String m1() { // covariant return (1.5+)
        return &quot;child&quot;;
    }
}</pre>
<h3>33:33 — Property 5: private, static, final methods</h3>
<p>Private methods can be overloaded. Static methods can be overloaded. Final methods can be overloaded.</p>
<p>You cannot override private, static, or final (private is not even visible as override; static is hiding; final is CE).</p>
<p>Overloading: can be overloaded — no restriction.</p>
<p>Overriding: cannot be overridden.</p>
<pre class='code'>class CanOverloadThese {
    private void m1() { }
    private void m1(int i) { } // private overloaded

    static void m2() { }
    static void m2(int i) { } // static overloaded

    final void m3() { }
    final void m3(int i) { } // final overloaded
}
class P {
    private void m1() { }
    static void m2() { }
    final void m3() { }
}

class C extends P {
    private void m1() { } // not overriding (parent private is not visible)
    static void m2() { }  // method hiding, not overriding
    // final void m3() { } // CE: m3() in C cannot override m3() in P; overridden method is final
}</pre>
<h3>34:24 — Property 6: access modifiers</h3>
<p>Overloading: first method private, second public — no problem. First protected, second public — no problem. No restrictions.</p>
<p>Overriding: while overriding, the scope of the access modifier cannot be reduced; if you want, you can increase.</p>
<pre class='code'>class OverloadAccessFree {
    private void m1(int i) { }
    public void m1(float f) { } // different args — access free
}
class P {
    public void m1() { }
}

class C extends P {
    // void m1() { } // CE: attempting to assign weaker access privileges; was public
    public void m1() { } // same public — OK
}
class P {
    void m1() { } // default
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1() { } // increased — OK
}</pre>
<h3>35:32 — Property 7: throws clause</h3>
<p>Overloading: no such restrictions. No restrictions.</p>
<p>Overriding: if a child class method throws any checked exception, compulsory parent class method should throw the same checked exception or its parent. No restrictions for unchecked exceptions.</p>
<p>(He says he will dictate this; do not worry about copying at speech speed.)</p>
<pre class='code'>class OverloadThrowsFree {
    public void m1(int i) { }
    public void m1(float f) throws Exception { } // different args — throws free
}
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.FileNotFoundException;

class P {
    public void m1() throws IOException { }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1() throws FileNotFoundException { } // child of IOException — OK
}
import java.io.IOException;

class P {
    public void m1() throws IOException { }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1() throws Exception { }
    // CE: m1() in C cannot override m1() in P
    // overridden method does not throw java.lang.Exception
}
class P {
    public void m1() { }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1() throws ArithmeticException { } // unchecked — OK
}</pre>
<h3>36:59 — Property 8: method resolution</h3>
<p>Overloading: always takes care by compiler based on reference type.</p>
<p>Overriding: always takes care by JVM based on runtime object.</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1(int... x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1(int x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;child&quot;);
    }
}

class ResolutionOverload {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p1 = new C();
        p1.m1(10);
        // prints parent — compiler, reference type P
    }
}
class P {
    public void m1() {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1() {
        System.out.println(&quot;child&quot;);
    }
}

class ResolutionOverride {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p1 = new C();
        p1.m1();
        // prints child — JVM, runtime object C
    }
}</pre>
<h3>37:46 — Property 9: also known as</h3>
<p>Overloading is also known as:</p>
<ul><li>compile-time polymorphism</li></ul>
<ul><li>or static polymorphism</li></ul>
<ul><li>or early binding</li></ul>
<p>(Method binding happens at compile time — early binding.)</p>
<p>Overriding is also known as:</p>
<ul><li>runtime polymorphism</li></ul>
<ul><li>or dynamic polymorphism</li></ul>
<ul><li>or late binding</li></ul>
<p>If any person asks the difference between overloading and overriding, tell these nine differences — “we can give left and right to that interview person.” All possible differences with respect to syntax.</p>
<h3>38:44 — Slow dictation of the full table</h3>
<p>He draws the table and dictates each cell. Wait until he finishes a point; he also adds extra theory while dictating.</p>
<p>Board table (final wording):</p>
<h3>47:56 — Conclusion note at the top of the table</h3>
<p>By seeing all nine rows, one simple conclusion:</p>
<p>In overloading we have to check only method names and argument types — names must be same, argument types must be different. Remaining things we are not required to check: return type, access modifier, throws keyword, etc.</p>
<p>In overriding everything we have to check — it is a complex process: method names, arguments, return types, access modifier, throws — complete prototype of the method.</p>
<p>Keep writing this note after the table:</p>
<p>In overloading we have to check only method names (must be same) and argument types (must be different). We are not required to check remaining like return types, access modifiers, etc.</p>
<p>But in overriding everything we have to check like method names, argument types, return types, access modifiers, throws clause, etc.</p>
<p>Overloading is the simple process. Overriding is complex because several things we have to take care of.</p>
<h3>51:46 — Exam-style question: parent method + which child methods are valid</h3>
<p>From this discussion, what can they ask in the exam? A simple example.</p>
<p>Parent class contains:</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.io.IOException;

class P {
    public void m1(int x) throws IOException {
    }
}</pre>
<p>In the corresponding child class, which methods can we take, and by which concept (overloading vs overriding) are they valid?</p>
<p>He writes five options:</p>
<pre class='code'>// 1
public void m1(int i)

// 2
public static int m1(long l)

// 3
public static void m1(int i)

// 4
public void m1(int i) throws Exception

// 5
public static abstract void m1(double d)</pre>
<h3>54:42 — Walk each option (he almost trips on option 2, then corrects)</h3>
<p>Option 1 — `public void m1(int i)`</p>
<p>Parent already has public void m1(int x) throws IOException. Same name, same argument. This is overriding. Child throws nothing — that is allowed (you may reduce checked exceptions). Valid by overriding.</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.io.IOException;

class P {
    public void m1(int x) throws IOException {
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1(int i) { // valid overriding
    }
}</pre>
<p>Option 2 — `public static int m1(long l)`</p>
<p>Room first says no: non-static to static not allowed; also void replaced with int not allowed — if it were overriding. Then he stops the confusion: 100% pakka it is overloading. Method names same, arguments different (int vs long). If arguments are different we are not required to check return type, static, access modifier. Valid by overloading. Don’t get confusion.</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.io.IOException;

class P {
    public void m1(int x) throws IOException {
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public static int m1(long l) { // valid overloading
        return 10;
    }
}</pre>
<p>Option 3 — `public static void m1(int i)`</p>
<p>Names same, arguments same → overriding. We are trying to override a non-static method with static. Non-static to static overriding is not possible. Invalid based on overriding rule.</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.io.IOException;

class P {
    public void m1(int x) throws IOException {
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public static void m1(int i) { }
    // CE: m1(int) in C cannot override m1(int) in P
    // overriding method is static
}</pre>
<p>Option 4 — `public void m1(int i) throws Exception`</p>
<p>Names same, arguments same → overriding. If child throws a checked exception, parent must throw the same checked exception or its parent. Here child throws Exception (parent of IOException), parent throws IOException (the child type). Wrong direction. Invalid based on overriding.</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.io.IOException;

class P {
    public void m1(int x) throws IOException {
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1(int i) throws Exception { }
    // CE: m1(int) in C cannot override m1(int) in P
    // overridden method does not throw java.lang.Exception
}</pre>
<p>Option 5 — `public static abstract void m1(double d)`</p>
<p>Looks like overloading (double vs int) so people mark it valid. Invalid. static and abstract is an illegal combination of modifiers. We cannot declare a static method as abstract / an abstract method as static. Compile error.</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.io.IOException;

class P {
    public void m1(int x) throws IOException {
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public static abstract void m1(double d);
    // CE: illegal combination of modifiers: abstract and static
}</pre>
<p>Maybe a chance to ask this type of question: consider this method in the parent; in the child, which of the following methods can we take?</p>
