<h3>1:01:37 — Now summarize under **polymorphism**</h3>
<p>That ends overloading vs overriding as a pair. Both are by default considered polymorphism. Keep writing the heading.</p>
<h3>1:02:16 — What is polymorphism? (Greek: many forms)</h3>
<p>Keep writing: polymorphism.</p>
<p>It is a Greek word. Poly means many, morphs means forms → many forms.</p>
<p>One name but multiple forms is the concept of polymorphism.</p>
<h3>1:02:42 — Life example: same person, different behaviour by place</h3>
<p>Best easy example: yourself.</p>
<ul><li>Inside the house, in front of parents: very innocent. Parents may feel “how will this person get on in life with this innocent nature.” Words at home: books, college, temple.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Outside, with friends: terminology changes. “Yesterday brand is not good, today we have to change the brand.” “Yesterday cinema release — first day first show only we have to see.”</li></ul>
<ul><li>Coming to Hyderabad from the village: “different colour” by default. People may wonder whether this is the same person from the village. Talk is Park, cinemas. Innocent nature completely gone.</li></ul>
<p>Person is the same, but based on the place the behaviour changes. Same person, multiple behaviours. That is polymorphism.</p>
<h3>1:04:38 — Java example 1: `abs` — same name, different argument types → overloading</h3>
<p>A method named abs. Same method name; you can apply for int, for long, for float. Same method, different argument types. Same but multiple forms → polymorphism. This concept is overloading. Overloading is under polymorphism.</p>
<pre class='code'>class AbsOverload {
    public int abs(int i) {
        return i &lt; 0 ? -i : i;
    }

    public long abs(long l) {
        return l &lt; 0 ? -l : l;
    }

    public float abs(float f) {
        return f &lt; 0 ? -f : f;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AbsOverload t = new AbsOverload();
        t.abs(10);
        t.abs(10L);
        t.abs(10.5f);
        // same name abs, three forms — overloading — polymorphism
    }
}</pre>
<h3>1:05:20 — Java example 2: `marry()` parent vs child → overriding</h3>
<p>Parent class: marry() implementation for Subbalakshmi (ASR: subal lakmi / subaki; any name, Appal, whatever parents may arrange).</p>
<p>Same method in the child: marry() implementation with whatever name you want — Trisha or Nayanthara or Charmi.</p>
<p>Method name / method signature is the same, implementations are different. Parent implementation meant for Subbalakshmi; child implementation meant for Nayanthara (etc.). That is overriding. Is it polymorphism? Yes.</p>
<p>Same signature: parent one type of implementation, child another type of implementation.</p>
<p>Overloading is an example of polymorphism. Overriding is an example of polymorphism.</p>
<p>One name but multiple forms is the concept of polymorphism.</p>
<h3>1:07:00 — Board dictation of the definition</h3>
<p>Keep writing:</p>
<p>Polymorphism: one name but multiple forms is the concept of polymorphism.</p>
<p>Example 1: method name is the same but we can apply for different types of arguments (overloading).</p>
<p>Take the example: abs(int) abs(long) abs(float) — overloading. Overloading is an example of polymorphism only.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Example1 {
    void abs(int i) { }
    void abs(long l) { }
    void abs(float f) { }
}</pre>
<p>Example 2: method signature is the same but in parent class one type of implementation and in the child class another type of implementation (overriding).</p>
<h3>1:09:56 — Board: `class P` `marry()` Subbalakshmi; `class C` `marry()` Charmi</h3>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void marry() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Subbalakshmi&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void marry() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Charmi&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<p>Any parent-arranged name is fine (Subbalakshmi / Appal). Child: fancy names, no problem — Charmi or Nayanthara. That is overriding. Overloading and overriding are the concept of polymorphism only.</p>
<h3>1:10:51 — Example 3: same `List` reference, different runtime objects</h3>
<p>Very commonly used. In collections there is List interface. Implementation classes: ArrayList, LinkedList, Vector, Stack. List is a child interface of Collection. (He will explain collections later; just use it now.)</p>
<p>We can use the same List reference to hold either ArrayList or LinkedList or Stack or Vector:</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Stack;
import java.util.Vector;

class ListParentRef {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List l1 = new ArrayList();
        List l2 = new LinkedList();
        List l3 = new Stack();
        List l4 = new Vector();
        // same List reference type; runtime object can be any List child
    }
}</pre>
<p>List l = new ArrayList() valid. new LinkedList() valid. new Stack() valid. new Vector() valid.</p>
<p>Reference is the same; runtime object can be anything. Usage of parent reference to hold any child-type object is also polymorphism.</p>
<h3>1:12:36 — Board: Example 3 wording + the four assignments</h3>
<p>Keep writing:</p>
<p>Example 3: usage of parent reference to hold child object is the concept of polymorphism.</p>
<p>Take the diagram (List with ArrayList, LinkedList, Vector, Stack under it) and the four lines:</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.*;

class Example3 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List l = new ArrayList();
        l = new LinkedList();
        l = new Stack();
        l = new Vector();
    }
}</pre>
<p>Wherever parent reference can be used to hold child object is the concept of polymorphism only.</p>
<h3>1:14:16 — Very important: **restriction** of parent reference (interview)</h3>
<p>Advantage of parent reference to hold child object = polymorphism, he can tell. But:</p>
<p>Parent p = new Child() is valid — parent reference can hold child object. By using that reference we can call only the methods available in the parent class. If we try to call a child-specific method, immediately compile-time error.</p>
<p>Assume parent contains m1, child contains m2.</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1() {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent m1&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m2() {
        System.out.println(&quot;child m2&quot;);
    }
}

class ParentRefRestriction {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p = new C(); // valid — parent ref holds child object
        p.m1();
        // prints parent m1
        p.m2();
        // CE: cannot find symbol
        //   symbol:   method m2()
        //   location: variable p of type P
    }
}</pre>
<p>He dictates the CE the way javac prints it: cannot find symbol — which symbol? method m2 — in which location? class P.</p>
<p>Very important: parent reference can be used to hold child object, but by using that reference we can call only methods available in the parent. Child-specific methods we cannot call.</p>
<p>If the parent class method is overridden in the child, then the child method will execute — but that is still a method that exists on the parent type. Methods which are only in the child, not in the parent, we cannot call through that reference.</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1() {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1() { // override — still callable via P
        System.out.println(&quot;child&quot;);
    }

    public void m2() { // child-specific — not callable via P
        System.out.println(&quot;m2&quot;);
    }
}

class OverrideVsChildSpecific {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p = new C();
        p.m1();
        // prints child  — overridden, runtime object C
        // p.m2(); // CE: cannot find symbol method m2() location: class P
    }
}</pre>
<h3>1:16:32 — Then what is the use? Child reference can call **both**</h3>
<p>Immediate question: then what is the use of giving the parent reference?</p>
<p>If we use child reference:</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1() {
        System.out.println(&quot;m1&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m2() {
        System.out.println(&quot;m2&quot;);
    }
}

class ChildRefBoth {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        C c = new C();
        c.m1(); // valid
        c.m2(); // valid
        // prints m1
        // prints m2
    }
}</pre>
<p>c.m1() valid, c.m2() valid. Then why parent reference? There is a restriction. Polymorphism benefit is there, but the problem is: by using parent reference we can call only methods available in parent class and we cannot call child-specific methods. In which case should we go for parent reference? He will explain after the board note.</p>
<h3>1:17:37 — Board dictation of the restriction</h3>
<p>Keep writing:</p>
<p>Parent class reference can be used to hold child object, but by using that reference we can call only the methods available in parent class and we can&#x27;t call child-specific methods.</p>
<p>Take this example and the diagram. Very important discussion — clear clarity.</p>
<h3>1:19:55 — Board code: `P p = new C();` `p.m1();` `p.m2();`</h3>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1() { }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m2() { }
}

class BoardParentRef {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p = new C();
        p.m1(); // valid
        p.m2(); // CE: cannot find symbol method m2() in class P
    }
}</pre>
<h3>1:20:16 — Child reference: both parent and child methods</h3>
<p>Keep writing:</p>
<p>But by using child reference we can call both parent and child class methods.</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1() { }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m2() { }
}

class BoardChildRef {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        C c = new C();
        c.m1(); // valid
        c.m2(); // valid
    }
}</pre>
<p>Both methods are valid.</p>
<h3>1:21:19 — When we should go for parent reference to hold child object</h3>
<p>Keep writing:</p>
<p>When we should go for parent reference to hold child object</p>
<p>Very simple: sometimes we don’t know what is the exact runtime object. If we don’t know the exact runtime type of object, happily we can go for parent reference.</p>
<h3>1:22:22 — Example 1: `ArrayList.get(0)` — return type is `Object`</h3>
<p>A List / ArrayList can hold any type of object (heterogeneous — he says they covered or will cover in collections). It may be Student object or Customer object or any object.</p>
<p>I want the first object: l.get(0) (ASR: “L do get Z” — zero index).</p>
<p>Zero-index object: it may be Student or Customer or String or StringBuffer — any type. How can you hold it? We don’t know exact runtime type — student, customer, string, integer, …</p>
<p>By using parent reference. That is why the return type of get present in ArrayList class is `Object`. Parent reference can hold any child object. In this type of situation parent reference is helpful to hold any type of runtime child-class object.</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.ArrayList;

class Student { }
class Customer { }

class GetZero {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList l = new ArrayList();
        l.add(new Student());
        l.add(new Customer());
        l.add(&quot;hello&quot;);
        l.add(new StringBuffer(&quot;hi&quot;));

        Object o = l.get(0);
        // get() return type is Object — we don&#x27;t know exact runtime type
    }
}</pre>
<p>Is parent-reference-to-hold-child an advantage feature? Yes. Use this approach if we don’t know runtime type of object.</p>
<h3>1:24:20 — Example 2: a method that may return ArrayList **or** LinkedList **or** Vector **or** Stack</h3>
<p>m1 based on some functionality may return ArrayList or LinkedList or Vector or Stack.</p>
<p>What return type to keep? If return type is ArrayList, we should return only ArrayList, not LinkedList/Vector/Stack.</p>
<p>We may return any of those four. Which return type covers all possibilities? `List`.</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Stack;
import java.util.Vector;

class ReturnAnyList {
    public List m1() {
        // based on functionality, any of these:
        return new ArrayList();
        // return new LinkedList();
        // return new Vector();
        // return new Stack();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ReturnAnyList t = new ReturnAnyList();
        List l = t.m1(); // parent ref — don&#x27;t know exact runtime type
    }
}</pre>
<p>Tomorrow you call m1(). It may be ArrayList or LinkedList or Vector or Stack. So you hold the result in a parent (List) reference.</p>
<p>If we don’t know exact runtime type → parent reference.</p>
<p>If we know exact runtime object — “it’s always going to return ArrayList only” — then use ArrayList reference only.</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.ArrayList;

class AlwaysArrayList {
    public ArrayList m1() {
        return new ArrayList();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AlwaysArrayList t = new AlwaysArrayList();
        ArrayList l = t.m1(); // we know exact runtime type
    }
}</pre>
<p>When parent reference vs child reference: if I know runtime type, hold with that particular reference. If I don’t know — runtime object can be any type — hold with common parent type.</p>
<h3>1:26:32 — Board: if we don’t know exact runtime type → parent reference</h3>
<p>Keep writing:</p>
<p>If we don’t know exact runtime type of object, then we should go for parent reference.</p>
<p>For example: the first element present in the ArrayList can be any type — it may be Student object or Customer object or String object or StringBuffer object. Hence the return type of get method is Object, which can hold any object.</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.ArrayList;

class BoardGetZero {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList l = new ArrayList();
        Object o = l.get(0);
    }
}</pre>
<p>If this facility were not there, how would you hold l.get(0)? You cannot, because you don’t know the runtime type. Usage of parent reference to hold child object is always an advantage feature. Make sure you are aware.</p>
<h3>1:29:32 — Interview pair: `C c = new C()` vs `P p = new C()`</h3>
<p>Summarize the differences. Very very important. A bit may be asked in the interview room.</p>
<pre class='code'>class P { }

class C extends P { }

class Pair {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        C c = new C();
        P p = new C();
    }
}</pre>
<p>More specifically they may ask:</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class PairList {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList l1 = new ArrayList();
        List l2 = new ArrayList();
    }
}</pre>
<p>What is the difference between ArrayList l = new ArrayList() and List l = new ArrayList()? Same question as C c = new C() vs P p = new C().</p>
<h3>1:30:55 — When for which approach</h3>
<p>Child / ArrayList reference: if we know exact runtime type of object (always ArrayList only, always Student only, always Customer only) → this approach.</p>
<p>Parent / List reference: if we don’t know exact runtime type of object → this approach.</p>
<h3>1:31:37 — Advantage / problem of each (three differences)</h3>
<p>Advantage of child reference: by using child reference we can call both parent and child class methods.</p>
<p>“Advantage” of parent reference (actually the restriction): by using parent reference we can call only parent class methods; child-specific methods we can’t call.</p>
<p>Problem with child reference: we can use child reference to hold only that particular child object. ArrayList reference can hold only ArrayList — not LinkedList.</p>
<p>Advantage of parent reference: we can use parent reference to hold any child object.</p>
<p>Polymorphism concept: advantage is there, problem is also there. Advantage: parent reference can hold any child object. Problem: by using that reference we can call only methods available in parent class.</p>
<p>Take the table. Very important for the interview room.</p>
<h3>1:33:28 — Table dictation: `C c = new C()` vs `P p = new C()`</h3>
<p>He also writes the ArrayList / List pair as the same table. Copy pause ~1:33:49.</p>
<p>Very important interview question: difference between P p = new C() and C c = new C(), or ArrayList l = new ArrayList() and List l = new ArrayList(). Observe advantage and disadvantage of both approaches, and when we should go for a particular approach.</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1() { }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m2() { }
}

class ChildWhenWeKnow {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        C c = new C(); // we know runtime type is C
        c.m1();
        c.m2();
        // can call both; can hold only C (not some other sibling)
    }
}
class P {
    public void m1() { }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m2() { }
}

class ParentWhenWeDontKnow {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p = new C(); // we don&#x27;t know exact child type
        p.m1();
        // p.m2(); // CE: cannot find symbol method m2() in class P
        // can hold any child of P
    }
}</pre>
<h3>1:39:17 — Three pillars of OOPs</h3>
<p>Summarize the three OOPs concepts. If you consider OOPs, three concepts act as pillars.</p>
<ol><li>Encapsulation — covered already (data hiding session).</li></ol>
<ol><li>Polymorphism — just now discussed.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Inheritance — covered already.</li></ol>
<h3>1:40:20 — One word each</h3>
<ul><li>Encapsulation always talks about one word: security.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Polymorphism always talks about one word: flexibility (flexibility to the programmer).</li></ul>
<ul><li>Inheritance always talks about one concept: reusability.</li></ul>
<p>These three are by default considered the three pillars of OOPs — basic important concepts of OOPs.</p>
<p>Keep writing the diagram:</p>
<p>Three pillars of OOPs</p>
<ul><li>Encapsulation</li></ul>
<ul><li>Polymorphism</li></ul>
<ul><li>Inheritance</li></ul>
<p>Long copy pause (~1:41:21–1:42:27). Then: encapsulation, polymorphism and inheritance.</p>
<h3>1:42:44 — A popular internet definition (he will share, with a disclaimer)</h3>
<p>There is one very popular definition for polymorphism circulated like anything on the internet. He will share it. Whether it is correct or not, he doesn’t know — he is not having any experience in that area.</p>
<p>Before that, one small summary table for polymorphism, then the funny definition.</p>
<h3>1:43:28 — Two types of polymorphism</h3>
<p>Polymorphism — two types:</p>
<p>1. Static polymorphism (or compile-time polymorphism, or early binding)</p>
<p>Two examples:</p>
<ul><li>overloading</li></ul>
<ul><li>method hiding (ASR: “method of hiding” — last session)</li></ul>
<p>Both are examples of compile-time / static polymorphism / early binding.</p>
<p>2. Dynamic polymorphism (or runtime polymorphism, or late binding)</p>
<p>Example: overriding.</p>
<pre class='code'>class StaticPolyOverload {
    public void m1(int i) { }
    public void m1(float f) { } // overloading — compile-time / early binding
}
class P {
    public static void m1() {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public static void m1() {
        System.out.println(&quot;child&quot;);
    }
}

class StaticPolyHiding {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p1 = new C();
        p1.m1();
        // prints parent — method hiding — compiler, reference type
    }
}
class P {
    public void m1() {
        System.out.println(&quot;parent&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m1() {
        System.out.println(&quot;child&quot;);
    }
}

class DynamicPolyOverride {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p1 = new C();
        p1.m1();
        // prints child — overriding — JVM, runtime object — late binding
    }
}</pre>
<h3>1:45:05 — Board summary table of polymorphism</h3>
<p>Keep writing clearly:</p>
<p>Polymorphism</p>
<ul><li>Static polymorphism or compile-time polymorphism or early binding</li></ul>
<p>Overloading and method hiding are examples</p>
<ul><li>Dynamic polymorphism or runtime polymorphism or late binding</li></ul>
<p>Example: overriding</p>
<h3>1:46:14 — “Beautiful definition of polymorphism” (friendship story)</h3>
<p>Keep writing: beautiful definition of polymorphism — but don’t ask whether it is correct or not; he is not having any experience in that. You can cross-check.</p>
<p>The word:</p>
<p>Your boy starts love with the word friendship. Usually gents start at bus stop or classroom: “why, we are just friends.” Then “the cinema is started.”</p>
<p>Girl ends love with the same word friendship. On wedding day she comes and gives a wedding card saying “we are friends forever.”</p>
<p>Whether this is correct or not, he doesn’t know. The word is the same; the attitude is different. One person used “friendship” to start; another person used the same word to end. This beautiful concept of OOPs is nothing but polymorphism.</p>
<p>If you want you can replace boy with girl or girl with boy — he doesn’t know. Whether it’s correct or not, also he doesn’t know. But this is very popular, circulated like anything on the internet, a very popular line about polymorphism / OOPs.</p>
<p>Better to take it whether you are accepting or not; if you are interested you can take, otherwise you can ignore.</p>
<p>Keep writing the punch line: word is the same but attitude is the different. Don’t ask whether it’s correct. Even he is not having any experience in that.</p>
<p>Copy pause ~1:48:19–1:48:43.</p>
<h3>1:48:44 — Polymorphism chapter ends; next topics</h3>
<p>That ends the concept of polymorphism.</p>
<p>Next they have to discuss: coupling, cohesion, type casting, next instance control flow, static control flow, followed by constructors.</p>
<h2>Board recap (what he wanted in the notebook)</h2>
<p>Var-arg: we can override a var-arg method with another var-arg method only. Parent m1(int... x) + child m1(int x) = overloading not overriding; compiler + reference type; output parent child parent. Replace child with m1(int... x) = overriding; output parent child child. Passing one argument does not change that — declared types still differ. Both-normal m1(int) would be real overriding.</p>
<p>Variables: overriding not applicable. P.x = 888, C.x = 999. Variable resolution always compiler based on reference type, irrespective of static or non-static. All four combinations print 888 999 888.</p>
<p>Overloading vs overriding (9): names same/same; args different (at least order) / same including order; signatures different/same; return no restriction / same until 1.4, covariant from 1.5; private static final can overload / cannot override; access no restriction / cannot reduce, can increase; throws no restriction / child checked ⇒ parent same or parent-type, unchecked free; resolution compiler+reference / JVM+runtime object; also known as compile-time/static/early vs runtime/dynamic/late. Overloading: check only names + args. Overriding: check everything.</p>
<p>Exam parent: public void m1(int x) throws IOException. Child: (1) void m1(int) valid overriding; (2) static int m1(long) valid overloading; (3) static void m1(int) CE overriding method is static; (4) void m1(int) throws Exception CE; (5) static abstract void m1(double) CE illegal combination abstract and static.</p>
<p>Polymorphism: one name, multiple forms. abs(int/long/float) = overloading. marry() Subbalakshmi vs Charmi = overriding. List holding ArrayList/LinkedList/Vector/Stack = parent ref holding child object.</p>
<p>Parent ref: can hold child; can call only parent methods; child-specific → CE cannot find symbol. Child ref can call both. Use parent ref when exact runtime type unknown (ArrayList.get(0) returns Object; method returning any List). Interview table: know type vs don’t; both methods vs parent-only; particular child vs any child.</p>
<p>Three pillars: encapsulation = security; polymorphism = flexibility; inheritance = reusability.</p>
<p>Poly types: static/compile-time/early = overloading and method hiding; dynamic/runtime/late = overriding.</p>
<p>Internet line (optional): boy starts love with “friendship”, girl ends love with “friendship” (“we are friends forever” on the wedding card). Word same, attitude different. He does not vouch for it.</p>
<p>Next: coupling, cohesion, type casting, instance control flow, static control flow, constructors.</p>
